WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.048 --> 00:00:04.718
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.718 --> 00:00:09.100
Disneyland is a paradox because
let's be honest it can be miserable.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.460
With long lines, expensive tickets and
kids having meltdowns.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.900
When our family went there a few years
ago my son Cooper later recalled

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.700
the experience in our annual holiday card
by recounting episodes of heat stroke and

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:22.720
vomiting.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.630
Followed by a long, silent car ride home.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:29.740
Yet he concluded his story by stating
quite sincerely, we can't wait to go back.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.000
Why is this?

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.880
How come when we look back on things like
family vacations you hardly remember

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.140
the sad or stressful moments, and
instead recall the happy ones?

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.760
One answer is laughter.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.160
It releases endorphins that
allow us to endure and

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.000
even triumph over negative emotions and
pain.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.270
Laughter and the ability to
find humor in bad situations

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:52.940
is what can explain the Disney Paradox.

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:57.100
Laughter helps to make negative
moments fade, but laughter's power,

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.360
extends well beyond making
amusement parks tolerable.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.300
Studies show that it can increase
creativity, improve health and

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.320
strengthen relationships.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.200
All of which can have a positive
effect in the business world as well.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.996
First let's look how humor
influences creativity.

00:01:12.996 --> 00:01:16.790
Psychologist Alice Eisen and
her colleagues asked participants to

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:20.860
watch a short video, and
then solve a classic creativity challenge.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.990
Where people are given a candle,
a box of tacks and some matches, and

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.070
then asked to attach the candle
to the wall in such a way

00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:30.910
that the wax doesn't drip on the table
below when the candle is lit.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:36.080
The answer is that you can use the box
containing the tacks to hold the candle.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.000
Half of the participants
watched a neutral video.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.719
The others watched a funny video.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.850
Only 20% of the participants in the
neutral condition solved the problem, but

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:48.990
three quarters of those in
the funny condition did.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.560
It's not that laughter made
the second group smarter.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.820
It's that it allowed them to relax more,
and feel emotionally safer.

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.240
Humor loosened up their mind, and

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.560
helped them see connections
they previously missed.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.960
Next let's look at health.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.370
In one study Michael Miller and his
colleagues showed a set of participants

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.050
two 15 minute movie
segments 48 hours apart.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:13.800
The first was a scene from the stressful
war movie Saving Private Ryan.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.360
Then two days later a clip from
the humorous comedy Kingpin.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.180
The participants' blood flow was
measured before the study, and

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.829
after each viewing session
with dramatic results.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.072
Blood flow decreased by 35%
after the stressful clip, and

00:02:28.072 --> 00:02:31.360
increased 22% after the funny one.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:34.200
Keep this in mind if your objective
is a better immune system.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:38.150
Finally, let's look at relationships.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.650
Doris Pazini and her colleagues recruited
couples to participate in a study.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:44.270
They asked some of the couples to
reminisce about times when they

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:45.550
laughed together.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:48.500
Other couples were asked to
reminisce about sharing memories

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.900
that made them feel good
about their relationship.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.110
At the end of the study,

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:56.030
the researchers measured how satisfied
the couples were in their relationship.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.380
The results showed the couples who
recall times when they laughed together

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:03.630
were more satisfied than those who
recalled shared positive memories.

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:06.140
Laughing together made
their hearts grow fonder.

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:09.780
So why is humor so powerful?

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.670
One reason is that laughter
releases endorphins which can

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:13.840
increase pain tolerance.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.240
In one study Robin Dunbar and his
colleagues tested the pain threshold of

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.220
participants by wrapping their arms
in a tightening blood pressure cuff

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.480
until the individual said they
couldn't take it anymore.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.770
Then they showed participants video clips.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:31.470
Half getting to see something funny,
and the other half a documentary.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.410
Pain tolerance increased by 10% for
the funny viewers.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.740
So humor seems to make us more
creative and healthier, and

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:41.990
it helps us to develop
stronger relationships.

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:45.060
Now this doesn't mean that you should show
clips from your favorite comedy to break

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.570
up a tense meeting.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:50.950
But it does mean that leaders should allow
humor to flourish whenever they can.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:53.230
Your teams will appreciate it.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:54.793
They may even say they
can't wait to go back.

00:03:54.793 --> 00:03:58.379
[MUSIC]

